From e328ef70bc2930cb779de1106a8daa515ac93d3c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tigerblue77 <37409593+tigerblue77@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 15:31:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] vdev_queue: adjust zfs_vdev_queue_window to read latency

blk-wbt is the only limit on writes to a disk that reacts to how long
reads take: it lets a quarter of min(16, queue depth) writes through
when a read was issued or completed in the last 100 ms, and about
halves that, down to 1, after each 100 ms in which every read took
longer than wbt_lat_usec, 75 ms on rotational disks.  With
zfs_vdev_disk_sync_writes=2, the writes the vdev queue paces leave
blk-wbt, and nothing replaces it: the queue keeps 2 to 10 async writes
active per leaf vdev by the amount of dirty data, whatever the reads.
On a Debian 13 VM with five null_blk rotational disks, that gave 1.5x
the writer throughput and 1.35x the read p99, the same as turning
blk-wbt off; on a raidz2 of five SATA HDDs, turning blk-wbt off made
reads about 20% slower.

So below the limit zfs_vdev_queue_window sets, keep a window per leaf
vdev: beyond its class's min_active, no I/O is issued while as many as
the window are active.  The window starts at the limit.  After each
period, 250 ms on rotating media and 20 ms on others, it shrinks by a
quarter, but not below 1, if more than a tenth of the reads and sync
writes completed during the period took longer than
zfs_vdev_queue_window_rotating_us (75 ms, blk-wbt's default for
rotational disks) or zfs_vdev_queue_window_non_rotating_us (2 ms, its
default for the others); otherwise, if it held an I/O back, it grows
by an eighth, up to the limit.  Sync writes count with reads because a
thread waits for them too, and they are slow when the disk is
congested; async writes complete into a volatile write cache, so their
latency says little.  The latency is io_delay, from issue to
completion, which zpool iostat reports as disk_wait.  A target of 0
leaves the window at the limit.

While reads are slow, the window holds async writes at
zfs_vdev_async_write_min_active, 2 by default, and scrub and the other
background classes at their min_active.  The sync classes keep theirs,
10, which is also their default max_active.  Their max_active still
applies: raised, it lets them use the room the window gives while
latency allows, and so absorb bursts of sync writes.

It is off by default.  Whether it protects reads as blk-wbt does while
keeping most of the throughput of zfs_vdev_disk_sync_writes=2 is what
it is for, and has not been measured yet.

Signed-off-by: Tigerblue77 <37409593+tigerblue77@users.noreply.github.com>
